Sat Sep 05 2026 Anton Isaiev <totoshko88@gmail.com> - 0.21.6

- Version bump to 0.21.6
- Added: an optional second confirmation before a snippet runs (#315) — a Confirm
  before running switch in the snippet editor, off by default so existing
  snippets keep running on a single action. It covers every route a snippet can
  be started from: the picker, the Execute button in the snippet manager, the
  variable-input dialog, the inline entries in the terminal's right-click menu,
  and the Scripts menu of an embedded RDP session
- Added: rustconn-cli snippet honours the same flag — --confirm on add, --confirm
  [true|false] on edit, and snippet run --execute prompts on stderr. With no
  terminal on stdin it refuses rather than prompting into a pipe; --force is the
  opt-out for scripts that mean it
- Fixed: Bitwarden auto-unlock did nothing in every interface language but
  English (#312). The vault state was compared as a translated display string
  against the literal "Locked", so outside English the guard read "not locked"
  and skipped the unlock. The state is an enum now and the decision is made on
  the variant, with the label rendered separately. It also fires on an
  inconclusive probe, which was a second route to the same silence
- Fixed: the startup banner announced that Bitwarden could not store passwords
  while Bitwarden was storing them (#312). The readiness probe ran bw status
  without BW_SESSION, so the CLI could not see the session and answered locked,
  correctly. The probe now builds its command the way the backend does
- Fixed: a Bitwarden password was written to the vault and reported as refused at
  the same time (#312). One store is three bw processes and a network round trip
  each; the ten-second budget expired mid-write while bw carried on and completed
  it. The budget is chosen per backend now, 45s for the four CLI-backed ones, and
  the message states the budget actually applied
- Fixed: every bw unlock ran without a deadline. Up to three child processes were
  spawned and none was bounded, so an unlock stalled on a network sync blocked
  its caller indefinitely. All four bw invocations now share one 30s ceiling. Two
  smaller things found alongside: the unlock logged the master password's length,
  and raw bw stderr could carry a session key into a log and into the error the
  Secrets page renders
- Fixed: Add SSH Key did nothing and there was no way to find out why. The file
  chooser's callback discarded every failure, so a dismissal, a real error and a
  callback that never fires were indistinguishable. Dismissal is now told apart
  from failure, and a second click supersedes the first request instead of
  leaving the button unable to open a chooser at all
- Fixed: the Add Key passphrase dialog had no visible way out — Cancel in the
  header now, and Enter in the passphrase field submits
- Fixed: global variables that could not be written to disk were reported as
  saved. The edits survived until the next start and then were simply gone
- Fixed: a standalone SSH tunnel could fail in complete silence while the
  diagnosis was being built and thrown away. A tunnel that exited on its own
  reports Failed rather than Stopped, with a warning icon and ssh's own words in
  a Last Error row. A failed start opens a dialog; a tunnel dying on its own
  raises a toast. The remedy names the package that is actually missing —
  mptcpize ships with the Multipath TCP tools, not with the OpenSSH client. The
  tunnel manager is also redrawn while it is open, which is where the failure was
  invisible before
- Fixed: running a snippet from the terminal's right-click menu could do nothing
  at all when a variable could not be resolved. It opens the variable dialog now,
  with whatever did resolve pre-filled
- Fixed: the embedded RDP confirmation showed the command untruncated. The
  terminal gate caps the preview at 400 characters because the dialog grows with
  its body until it stops being readable; the RDP gate passed the command whole,
  so a generated one-liner made the dialog unreadable on exactly the snippets a
  confirmation is worth having for
- Fixed: RUST_LOG=debug wrote the RDP account password into the log in clear.
  sspi logs the encoded CredSSP TSRequest at debug level, and field [2] of
  TSPasswordCreds is the password as plain UTF-16LE, so the log carried a
  decodable byte array — while the same line printed "password: Secret" in its
  span fields, the wrapper working perfectly on the field RustConn owns. An
  sspi=warn directive now joins the three ironrdp ones, added after
  EnvFilter::from_default_env() so RUST_LOG cannot re-enable it. Anyone who ran
  0.21.5 or earlier with RUST_LOG=debug against an RDP host should treat that
  account's password as disclosed
- Fixed: a clean shutdown reported that every terminal child had ignored SIGTERM.
  still_our_group_leader answers true for a zombie, and on shutdown that is the
  normal state — the reaper is GLib's child watch and its main loop is gone by
  then, so a child that obeyed promptly is still in the process table when the
  grace period ends. The check reads state Z from /proc/<pid>/stat now and both
  escalation sites say which they found. The SIGKILL to the process group stays
  in either branch, since an ssh with a ProxyCommand shares its group with
  helpers the parent's exit does not reach; only the message was wrong
- Fixed: an unset console keymap made every RDP session US English. localectl
  status prints VC Keymap above X11 Layout, and on a desktop the console keymap
  is normally unconfigured, so the parser returned "(unset)" and fell back to US
  English with the layout it wanted on the next line. The server then interprets
  every scancode against the wrong table, so a German or French keyboard types
  the wrong characters with nothing in the UI pointing at the cause. X11 Layout
  now wins wherever it appears, the placeholders systemd prints for an unset
  value are rejected rather than read as layout names, and both sources report
  the whole group list
- Fixed: with LibSecret, a password saved on a connection in no group was never
  found again (#316). generate_store_key_with_group takes an Option<&str> group
  path with three meanings, and four call sites built it themselves: saving and
  deleting passed Some(""), while resolving and the connection dialog's load and
  test buttons passed None. So the password went to RustConn/{name} ({protocol})
  and was looked for at {name} ({protocol}), and the Secret Service matches
  attributes exactly. A grouped connection escaped it because there the two keys
  differ and the legacy second lookup hits. The decision now lives in one
  function, generate_store_key_for_connection, taking the group id rather than a
  pre-joined path, and save, resolve, delete, migrate and the credential transfer
  all call it. Four tests pin the GUI's key against the resolver's
- Fixed: the shutting-down flag was set after the session exits it exists to
  explain. APP_SHUTTING_DOWN was set from connect_shutdown, which GTK runs from
  app.quit() — after shutdown_sessions_for_exit has already signalled every
  session child — so on the ordinary quit path both guards that ask the question
  got false, and the one that suppresses a reconnect banner and a failure toast
  for a session the teardown just killed was unreachable. It is set at the top of
  that teardown now, which every exit funnels through and a tray-minimize never
  reaches. The post-disconnect command is also skipped at shutdown, and says so,
  instead of being started on a worker thread the process exits from under with
  nothing able to log the outcome
- Fixed: a second Quit stacked a second confirmation instead of raising the
  first. close_confirmation_dialog is reached from the window's close_request and
  from the app.quit action, where Ctrl+Q, the primary menu and the tray's Quit
  item all arrive, and neither asked whether the question was already on screen —
  so the first click looked ignored while its dialog sat behind another window or
  hidden to the tray. Both go through one helper now, which returns the dialog to
  wire up or raises the window carrying the pending one; nothing is ever read as
  consent, and the quit action logs which of the three outcomes happened
- Improved: the four hand-rolled bw unlock invocations on the Secrets page are
  gone; they all call one core function that has the extended PATH, the deadline
  and the verbose fallback
- Improved: one confirmation prompt in rustconn-cli instead of three. The copies
  differed in whether a non-interactive stdin is a silent abort or an error
  naming --force, which a bool cannot carry; the shared helper reports three
  outcomes and never treats "nobody to ask" as consent
- Improved: OpenH264 was probed again on every RDP connection. The embedded
  client dlopens a decoder rather than linking one, and each connection rewalked
  the whole candidate list from scratch, re-emitting the same loader warnings —
  three connections in one log produced nine identical lines about a distribution
  libopenh264 failing the hash check, at the one severity users actually read.
  The outcome is cached now, the path that loaded or nothing, and a fresh decoder
  is built from it per session; a failure to build one from an already-proven
  path is a warning rather than a silent fall back to RemoteFX. The trade is that
  installing a Cisco blob into a running RustConn is not picked up until restart
- Improved: a KeePass lookup paid for one database open that could never match.
  Connecting to a host whose password is not in the database took 2.9 s before
  the prompt appeared: three keepassxc-cli invocations at roughly 700 ms each,
  since every run reopens the KDBX and pays its Argon2 cost again. For a grouped
  connection one candidate was the un-prefixed path, which build_entry_path has
  never written — it can only match the ungrouped case, and there it stays. The
  construction moved into a pure function so the order is pinned by tests rather
  than by reading it, and find_keepassxc_cli remembers a successful find instead
  of redoing a PATH walk — plus, inside a Flatpak sandbox, a host child process —
  for every reader and writer in the module
- Documentation: three claims that were not true of the code —
  docs/CLI_REFERENCE.md said snippet run resolves ${VARIABLE} from Global
  Variables before execution, which it cannot, since a global may be vault-backed
  and the CLI has no session to unlock it with; rustconn-cli/AGENTS.md required
  every user-facing string to go through i18n() in a crate that has never
  contained one, so the rule was followed by nobody and following it once would
  have produced output in two languages; and the comment listing the routes to
  the snippet picker omitted the terminal context menu's own entry, which is that
  action's main caller
- Documentation: the debug-logging instructions did not say what a debug log
  discloses. docs/USER_GUIDE.md told users to run with RUST_LOG=debug and attach
  the output to a bug report, which names every host, username and connection
  opened, and until the leak fixed above carried the RDP account password itself.
  The warning now comes before the command rather than after it, while there is
  still a decision to make
- Dependencies: cc 1.4.4-1.4.5, find-msvc-tools 0.1.11-0.1.12, indexmap
  2.14.1-2.14.2, js-sys 0.3.104-0.3.105, syn 3.0.4-3.0.5, tinyvec 1.12.0-1.13.2,
  tokio-rustls 0.26.4-0.26.5, wasm-bindgen 0.2.127-0.2.128 together with its
  -futures, -macro, -macro-support and -shared crates, web-sys 0.3.104-0.3.105,
  zstd-safe 7.2.4-7.3.0, zstd-sys 2.0.16-2.1.0. Six of the fifteen are the
  wasm-bindgen family, and nothing this project ships compiles them: cargo tree
  --invert finds no path to wasm-bindgen on the host target, so they are
  reachable only behind a wasm32 target and are recorded because Cargo.lock
  moved, not because a binary did. indexmap is the one with real reach, arriving
  three ways — through h2/hyper/reqwest, through serde_yaml_ng, and through
  toml_edit behind the GTK macro crates. Thirteen further crates remain behind
  their latest release on semver grounds, a count this run leaves unchanged, and
  cargo deny check advisories is clean against the new lockfile
- Dependencies: FreeRDP (Flatpak) 3.31.0-3.31.1. Upstream calls it a papercut
  release, so no advisory behind it, but it fixes flickering in the SDL3 client on
  displays scaled other than 1.0, maps keys that client used to drop, and makes
  WinPR read JSON files in binary mode — the sdl-freerdp.json path the bundled
  cJSON module exists to enable. Only Flatpak/Flathub bundle FreeRDP; this package
  uses the distribution's copy and is unaffected
- Dependencies: openh264 2.6.0 is now bundled in all three Flatpak manifests, not
  just the release one. The embedded RDP client dlopens a decoder rather than
  linking one, so the Flathub build had none at all and every AVC420 or AVC444
  session negotiated down to RemoteFX; a local flatpak-builder run had none
  either. Does not affect this package
- Dependencies: the nine other bundled Flatpak modules were checked against
  upstream and are already current, so only FreeRDP moved

