Tue Sep 01 2026 Anton Isaiev <totoshko88@gmail.com> - 0.21.3
- Version bump to 0.21.3
- Fixed: a password the selected backend refused was moved into the encrypted
  file without asking, where the connect path never looks. Saving walked the
  fallback chain and wrote to credentials.enc, while connect-time resolution
  queries the selected backend alone — so a locked vault produced a password that
  was saved and, to the connection, missing. Saving now targets the selected
  backend only; a refusal opens a dialog naming the backend and offers the
  encrypted file as a deliberate choice. Reads still walk the chain so pre-switch
  passwords keep resolving
- Fixed: secret backends disagreed on how to report a failure, and three of them
  called an unreadable store a missing entry. KeePassXC turned every non-zero
  keepassxc-cli exit except invalid credentials into "entry not found"; pass
  treated any non-zero pass show as a miss and had no readiness check; and the
  KeePass resolve path fell through to the generic chain, so a locked database
  could serve a password from libsecret or the encrypted file. All three now
  separate "opened, entry absent" from "did not open" and answer like every other
  backend
- Fixed: secret status was reported with the wrong words, the wrong store, or not
  at all — "Secret Backend Not Configured" shown to a locked backend, a startup
  banner describing every backend as a keyring, Passbolt and Pass stuck on
  "Detecting…", and the pass readiness probe reading a different store than the
  backend uses. Each now reports the backend in force and whether it can store a
  password
- Fixed: the Bitwarden master password was demoted out of its Zeroizing wrapper
  before use, leaving copies in freed heap; both copies stay wrapped now, and the
  session-key length is no longer logged
- Fixed: an embedded RDP session against a Windows 11 host could be killed by the
  server's own keepalive (#262). ironrdp-session decodes every PDU on the MCS
  message channel as an auto-detect request, but that channel also carries the
  Heartbeat PDUs Windows 11 sends every one to two seconds; the first failed to
  decode and the session ended as a plain "Session error". A PDU the session layer
  cannot decode is now survivable when it arrived on the message channel, while a
  failure to parse the MCS framing itself stays fatal
- Fixed: an idle window with no connections showed network warnings during a
  Wi-Fi flap, the first reading "active sessions may be interrupted" when there
  were none. All transition paths now announce only when something is at stake,
  counted through the open session count; the socket sweeps still run on every
  transition
- Changed: "Enable fallback" is now "Also read from the encrypted file" and
  governs reads only — where a write goes is decided at save time, per the fix
  above
- Changed: the Secrets page reports whether the selected backend can actually
  store a password, with one Status line per backend, re-checked after settings
  are saved. SecretManager::retrieve_reported now reports which backend answered a
  read
- Documentation: the Bitwarden guide, architecture, user guide and build docs
  updated for the read/write asymmetry and for where the Bitwarden CLI keeps its
  login state under Flatpak (#312, #129)
- Dependencies: libredox 0.1.21→0.1.23, ppmd-rust 1.4.0→1.4.1, smallvec
  1.15.2→1.16.0, all semver-compatible transitive updates from cargo update

